---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

interface Props {
  versioned?: boolean;
}

const { versioned = false } = Astro.props;
const currentPath = versioned ? "/methodology/1.0.0" : "/methodology";
const title = versioned
  ? "Methodology v1.0.0 — QuantClarity"
  : "Methodology — QuantClarity";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description="How QuantClarity defines precision, groups models, evaluates evidence, handles freshness and prices, and preserves neutral ordering."
  currentPath={currentPath}
>
  <article class="prose shell">
    <header class="page-header flush">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methodology version 1.0.0 · Effective 2026-08-01</p>
      <h1>How QuantClarity handles facts</h1>
      <p class="lede narrow">
        QuantClarity publishes scoped, evidence-backed facts. It does not rank
        or recommend inference providers.
      </p>
      {
        !versioned && (
          <p>
            The stable record of this methodology is available at{" "}
            <a href="/methodology/1.0.0">version 1.0.0</a>.
          </p>
        )
      }
    </header>

    <h2>Precision vocabulary and scope</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Source-checkpoint precision</dt>
      <dd>
        The declared weight format of an identified upstream checkpoint. A
        checkpoint may be canonical, publisher-provided quantized, or a
        separately evidenced third-party conversion.
      </dd>
      <dt>Source-provided quantization</dt>
      <dd>
        A quantized checkpoint or variant published or explicitly endorsed by
        the original model publisher.
      </dd>
      <dt>Serving-weights precision</dt>
      <dd>
        The stored weight representation for an exact provider offering, tier,
        endpoint class, and material region established by evidence.
      </dd>
      <dt>Compute and component precision</dt>
      <dd>
        Computation formats are recorded separately for weight computation,
        activations, accumulation, KV cache, attention, experts, shared layers,
        and other explicitly named components. A known component never fills an
        unknown sibling component.
      </dd>
      <dt>Normalized labels</dt>
      <dd>
        BF16, FP16, FP8, FP6, FP4, NVFP4, MXFP4, INT8, and INT4 retain their
        exact labels. Format details such as FP8 E4M3/E5M2 and block formats are
        retained when supplied. BF16 and FP16 are distinct; neither is assigned
        a universal quality advantage.
      </dd>
      <dt>Mixed, other, and unknown</dt>
      <dd>
        Mixed preserves component details; other preserves an evidenced format
        outside the current vocabulary; unknown means no supported exact-scope
        fact is available. Names, speed, price, accelerators, reputation, and a
        served model's self-description are never substitutes for evidence.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      A wider downstream format does not restore information discarded by a
      lower-precision source checkpoint. Expanding INT4 or FP8 source weights
      into BF16 does not recreate discarded source information.
    </p>

    <h2>Model grouping</h2>
    <p>
      A provider's serving precision alone does not split a canonical model. An
      explicitly named and selectable precision or material variant receives its
      own variant resource and links to its canonical family. Alias matching
      never hides an explicit variant distinction, and canonical and variant
      provider comparisons remain separate.
    </p>

    <h2>Evidence and source precedence</h2>
    <p>
      Precedence is field-specific, not simply “newest wins.” Exact provider API
      or authenticated-catalog facts lead for the exact offering, followed by
      exact provider-controlled public documentation. Publisher-controlled
      checkpoints and documentation lead for upstream identity, architecture,
      and checkpoint facts. Exact provider support or changelog statements may
      follow. Independent structured catalogs support discovery and conflict
      detection; community discussion is only a lead for investigation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Applicability and verification are checked before precedence or recency.
      Equally authoritative unresolved conflicts publish as unknown. Every
      non-null fact identifies evidence and an observation time; lower-priority
      conflicts cannot silently overwrite a winning claim.
    </p>

    <h2>Freshness and status</h2>
    <p>
      An offering becomes stale after two consecutive completed Monday/Thursday
      refresh opportunities without a successful exact-offering observation, or
      after more than eight elapsed days since its last successful
      observation—whichever happens first. A run still within its 12-hour
      terminal deadline is not yet missed. Stale active and historical offerings
      remain visible through explicit filters, while the default table is
      limited to active, non-stale offerings.
    </p>

    <h2>Prices</h2>
    <p>
      Input, output, and cached-input prices stay separate; a missing cached
      input price is unknown, never zero. The provider-stated currency is
      preserved without foreign-exchange conversion. If a provider omits
      currency, USD is visibly labeled as a system default rather than a
      provider statement.
    </p>
    <p>
      Standard comparable prices are generally available, on-demand,
      pay-as-you-go rates without subscriptions, commitments, volume minimums,
      batch conditions, or dedicated deployments. Conditional, subscription,
      batch, volume, committed-use, context-tiered, region-tiered, “from,” and
      promotional prices remain visible with their exact qualifiers, but do not
      replace or enter the default standard-price sort unless explicitly
      included. Numeric price sorting always uses one visible currency scope.
    </p>

    <h2>Neutral comparison and ordering</h2>
    <p>
      The default offering order is provider display name ascending, then stable
      offering ID ascending. Users may explicitly sort by provider, normalized
      precision label, each standard comparable price role, freshness, or
      status. Precision display order is organizational only—not fidelity,
      quality, or lineage ranking—and mixed, other, and unknown remain explicit.
      Affiliate status and commission never affect facts, inclusion, relevance,
      filters, or ordering.
    </p>

    <h2>Material-change log</h2>
    <p>
      Version 1.0.0 establishes the initial precision vocabulary, model-grouping
      rules, evidence precedence, freshness policy, price treatment, and neutral
      comparison behavior. Future material changes will receive a new stable
      version and an entry here; historical versions will remain available.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
